WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.660
After creating and running Saturday Night
Live for five seasons, Lorne Michaels stepped

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.930
away from the sketch comedy to work on some
other TV projects.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:12.190
As a result, the show's sixth season, which
ran from 1980 to 1981, turned out to be an

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.330
incredibly tumultuous year for the creatives
on SNL.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:19.220
Although much of the chaos ensued behind the
scenes, there were some on-screen shifts that

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.730
rendered the show's comedy pretty forgettable.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:26.399
So forgettable, in fact, that people rarely
bring up any moments from this season at all

00:00:26.399 --> 00:00:29.860
unless they're talking about how disastrously
bad it was.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.950
Here's what you might not know about Saturday
Night Live's forgotten season.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:35.430
Breaking away

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:39.740
After five years of putting in the intense
time and effort it takes to make SNL shine,

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:43.790
Lorne Michaels decided he needed to take a
step back from the executive chair for a bit.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:47.820
At first, he just wanted the summer off and
to delay the sixth season premiere to just

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.430
after the 1980 election, instead of the usual
September start.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:55.090
But Michaels also admitted he wasn't sure
if he wanted to come back at all, because

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.280
to continue the show meant he'd have to sacrifice
other ambitions.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.570
So, he floated some ideas to NBC about who
should take over in his absence just in case.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:07.970
His top choice was for SNL writers and occasional
performers Al Franken and Tom Davis to take

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:08.970
over.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.861
He figured they'd be the best candidates to
maintain the quality, integrity, and tone

00:01:11.861 --> 00:01:14.770
of the show, since they'd all worked together
for so long.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.000
But he doomed Franken's chances before the
possibility could even materialize.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.799
Michaels had been scheduled to meet with NBC
president Fred Silverman to discuss their

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:24.479
future, but Silverman had to postpone.

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:28.720
Then, the weekend before the rescheduled appointment,
Franken appeared on a segment of "Weekend

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.150
Update" that specifically targeted the head
honcho.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:36.970
"You know who gets complete, door-to-door
limousine service from NBC?

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:38.170
Fred Silverman."

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:41.590
Franken said in the piece that it wasn't fair
that he should have to hail cabs in New York

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:46.100
while Silverman got to take a limousine wherever
he wanted, even while NBC was failing in the

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:46.900
ratings.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.100
"I like to call it a limo for the lame-o."

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.530
Silverman saw the episode — and lost his
cool with Michaels.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.630
The relationship between Michaels and the
network soured so badly that Michaels decided

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:01.960
to walk away from SNL completely, and Silverman
overlooked Franken as Michaels' replacement.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:03.180
Regrouping

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:07.600
A few weeks after his spat with NBC brass,
Michaels got a call from another exec who

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.830
informed him that his successor would be Jean
Doumanian, an associate producer for the show.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:17.120
Michaels thought Doumanian was, quote, "an
interesting choice" because he felt SNL was,

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.590
quote, "a writer-based show," and Doumanian
had been on the writing staff for exactly

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.700
one TV variety special.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.620
But her years on SNL did give her the knowledge
of how the show was assembled each week, and

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.290
Doumanian's skills as a talent coordinator
would come in handy.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:34.519
That's because when Michaels left, so did
the entire cast of Saturday Night Live: including

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:39.180
Jane Curtin, Bill Murray, Garrett Morris,
Laraine Newman, and Gilda Radner.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.110
So that meant Doumanian had to assemble an
entirely new cast.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:46.980
She put together a roster that included Denny
Dillon, Gilbert Gottfried, Joe Piscopo, and

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:47.980
Eddie Murphy.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.809
Although Murphy was not the first choice for
the new roster, he continuously contacted

00:02:51.809 --> 00:02:55.920
talent coordinator Neil Levy until he gave
the comedian a chance to audition.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.040
Little did anyone know that Murphy's audition
routine would come in handy later on in the

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.040
show.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.260
See, almost every writer left with Lorne Michaels
and the cast, so Doumanian had to recruit

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.420
a new stable of talent behind-the-scenes too.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.719
But with the writer's room becoming its own
sort of revolving door while she was in charge,

00:03:11.719 --> 00:03:13.359
there was a shortage of sketches.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:17.370
In one case, they were running five minutes
short on material and decided at the last-minute

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.219
to have Murphy go out and perform his stand-up
routine to fill the empty airtime.

00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:25.959
The 19-year-old became the actual saving grace
of the entire season after that.

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:27.609
Multiple missteps

00:03:27.609 --> 00:03:32.469
Despite the introduction of Murphy as a rising
star, the sixth season of SNL was still filled

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:33.840
with snafus.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.310
For example, In the cold open of the season
premiere, host Elliott Gould wakes up in bed

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:42.819
with some new cast members, and they're immediately
compared to the show's former cast members.

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:47.329
"Sort of a cross between Gilda and Jane."

00:03:47.329 --> 00:03:52.489
In other words, the show's new era began with
an unfavorable comparison to its old iteration,

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:54.769
and it was a steep slide down from there.

00:03:54.769 --> 00:03:59.169
In one episode, some good ol' boys try to
shoot Communists, and one character used the

00:03:59.169 --> 00:04:00.169
N-word.

00:04:00.169 --> 00:04:03.519
But the season wasn't just offensive — it
was also boring and bizarre.

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:06.870
There was a five-minute-long sketch about
a couple who can't remember the name of their

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:07.870
friend's cat.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.139
"The Leather Weather Report" featured Denny
Dillon as a dominatrix named Thelma Thunder

00:04:12.139 --> 00:04:14.280
giving her S&amp;M-themed forecast.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.130
Perhaps most idiotically, "Weekend Update"
was ditched in favor of a failed segment called

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:20.480
"Saturday Night Newsline."

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.850
And in what became the most infamous incident
of the season, on episode 11 in February,

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:29.150
cast member Charles Rocket dropped an eff-bomb
on live air.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:33.700
“Charlie, How are you feeling after you’ve
been shot?”

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:36.100
“Oh man, it’s the first time I’ve ever
been shot in my life.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.080
I’d like to know who the f--- did it.”

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.460
The show lost 30 percent of its audience,
and critics maligned the new direction.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:46.810
The New York Times even called it "an unfunny
parody of its predecessor" that relied too

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.180
heavily on racial and sexual humor.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:53.770
If the execs wants to salvage their hit series,
they'd have to think fast.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:55.510
Beginning again

00:04:55.510 --> 00:05:00.420
After the 12th episode of the season — one
after Charles Rocket's eff-word incident — Doumanian

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.820
was relieved from her position as SNL producer.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:07.080
Her replacement was NBC executive Dick Ebersol,
who'd actually helped develop Saturday Night

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.050
Live in the mid-'70s.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:13.000
Immediately after taking over, Ebersol fired
most of the cast, including Charles Rocket,

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.600
Gilbert Gottfried, Ann Risley, Yvonne Hudson,
Matthew Laurance, and Patrick Weathers.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.360
In their places, he brought in SCTV stars
Robin Duke and Tony Rosato, as well as Tim

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:23.440
Kazurinsky.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.210
Laurie Metcalf and National Lampoon editor
Emily Prager were also hired as featured players.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:33.150
By the time the season was finished, Ebersol
also fired Gail Matthius and Denny Dillon,

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:37.170
so the only Doumanian hires to survive the
season were breakout stars Eddie Murphy and

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:38.230
Joe Piscopo.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:42.280
Thanks to Murphy's status as the MVP of the
season, he became the first featured player

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:46.610
promoted to regular cast member during his
first season on the show.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:48.040
Bad timing

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.300
What set all these massive changes into motion
was producer Lorne Michaels wanting a break

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:53.840
in the fall of 1980.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.180
Ironically, had he just stayed with the show
and said nothing to NBC, he probably would've

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:59.180
gotten one.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:04.100
The sixth season of SNL didn't actually air
its first episode until November 15th, 1980

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:07.440
— about two months late — all because
of a Screen Actors Guild strike.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.770
But luckily for everyone involved with the
disaster-plagued season, it also ended early,

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.390
cut short abruptly after the 13th episode
due to a writers strike.

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:19.930
The 14th episode had already been planned,
and it might have helped get SNL back on track.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:23.950
As a guest on Weekend Update, would-be showrunner
Al Franken announced he'd be back the next

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:28.450
week to host and offered a scathing monologue
about how he was hellbent on repairing the

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:29.450
broken season.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:34.380
"Anyway it took NBC 12 shows to figure out
their horrendous mistake."

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:38.260
But the strike-shortened season led to some
of the shortest tenures in SNL history.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:42.530
Featured players Laurie Metcalf and Emily
Prager were let go after that de facto season

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:47.230
finale, and Metcalf only appeared in one pre-taped
bit for Weekend Update, while Prager never

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.220
officially appeared on the show at all.

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:53.570
Thanks to the brevity of the season and so
many cast and creative shake-ups, it's hardly

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:57.460
any wonder why SNL's sixth season hasn't shown
up much in the highlight reel.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:00.230
But, hey — at least it introduced the world
to Eddie Murphy.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:01.340
Thanks for watching!

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:03.680
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.570
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

